<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bristol</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bristol is a city and port in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on the River Avon, near its mouth on the Severn estuary. A major trading town since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, it sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-cabot">John Cabot</a>'s expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> in 1497. In the eighteenth century it was one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s leading ports in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> and the trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and tobacco, and it later became an industrial centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bristol.jpg" alt="Bristol" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chris Lathom-Sharp · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clifton_Suspension_Bridge_and_the_Observatory_in_Bristol,_England.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
